Code page tables (internal/codepage/tables.go)
----------------------------------------------

The tables of the single-byte code pages are the content of the mapping
files published by the Unicode Consortium, written out as Go arrays:

  Windows 874, 1250, 1251, 1252, 1253, 1254, 1255, 1256, 1257, 1258:
    https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP874.TXT
    https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP1250.TXT
    ... through CP1258.TXT
  Mac OS Roman (code page 10000):
    https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/APPLE/ROMAN.TXT

The files are provided by the Unicode Consortium and the vendors named in
them (Microsoft Corporation, Apple Inc.) under the Unicode terms of use,
which permit use of the mapping data. Bytes the files leave undefined are
undefined in the tables (decoded as U+FFFD with a warning).

The tables were transcribed while go-fast-rtf was written and have since
been compared byte by byte with Python's codecs of the same code pages
(which implement these mapping files), with no difference.
codepage_test.go checks the structure of each table and a sample of values.

Double-byte code page tables (internal/codepage/cp932.txt, cp936.txt,
cp949.txt, cp950.txt)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Generated by internal/codepage/gen/gen_dbcs.py from Python's codecs cp932,
cp936, cp949 and cp950, which implement Microsoft's Windows code pages as
published by the Unicode Consortium:

    https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP932.TXT
    ... CP936.TXT, CP949.TXT, CP950.TXT

Mappings to C1 controls (U+0080 to U+009F) and to the Private Use Area
(U+E000 to U+F8FF), which the codecs add beyond the published files, are
left out of the tables (see the generator's header). The decoder
(internal/codepage/codepage.go) computes some of them as Windows maps
them: in 932, byte 0x80 alone to U+0080 and lead bytes 0xF0 to 0xF9 to
U+E000 to U+E757; in 936, the three user-defined areas to U+E000 to
U+E765. Other such bytes are decoded as U+FFFD with a warning. The first
line of each file names the Python version that generated it. The mapping
data is used under the Unicode terms of use.


Test corpus
===========

The files of the test corpus are not part of this repository. testdata/corpus.json
lists each by URL and hash, with the licence it is under at its source;
testdata/fetch.py downloads them.
